  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Silverleaf fungus (Chondrostereum purpureum) is a wood-decay fungus that causes a silvery discoloration of leaves on affected trees and shrubs, making it economically significant in New England orchards and woodlands. This pathogenic fungus spreads through fresh wounds on trees and is often used as a biological control agent against unwanted hardwood species.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Fruit Body Appearance",
      "description": "Produces thin, purple-tinged, fan-shaped or bracket-like fruiting bodies on dead wood, typically 1-3 cm across, with a white or pale underside",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Silvery Leaf Discoloration",
      "description": "Causes distinctive silvery or grayish appearance on affected leaves as the fungus blocks water transport in the plant's vascular system",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Wood Host Preference",
      "description": "Commonly found on hardwood species including apple, cherry, birch, and other deciduous trees in New England forests",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Silverleaf fungus thrives on freshly cut or wounded hardwood in New England's temperate forests, orchards, and disturbed areas, preferring moist conditions.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "This fungus is commercially cultivated and applied as a biological herbicide to control invasive hardwoods and unwanted tree species, making it both a pest and a useful management tool.",
